WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.751
Hey, guys. Welcome back to the Channel.

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:02.502
If you're new here,

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:06.965
my name's Dami, and I am an architect
in beautiful Vancouver, B.C..

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:10.093
Today, we're going to be doing something
a little bit different.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:14.848
We are going to be looking
at a couple of my favorite sci fi movies

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:17.934
that have really interesting
architectural concepts.

00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:20.687
This video is sponsored by HP.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.356
We'll talk about my keyboard
set up later in this video.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:27.485
So the very first movie
that we're watching is Ex Machina,

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:28.903
which is a great movie.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:30.572
If you guys haven't watched it.

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:33.491
And the storyline is about this,
a software engineer.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.412
He wins a lottery to do an experiment

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:41.624
at the House of this,
his CEO of his company,

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:45.795
who's the cross between Jeff
Bezos and Elon Musk and Steve Jobs.

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:49.299
And he created this A.I., this Android.

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:54.220
And the main character
is basically going to his house to perform

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.266
the modern version of a Turing test
to see if this A.I.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.351
can pass as a human.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:03.313
Everything takes place in one house.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:06.608
We'll kind of go over the house
in a little bit,

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:09.402
but it's a really fascinating movie
if you haven't watched it.

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:13.490
So he arrives at this very foreign
landscape.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:18.161
He's already kind of thrown
into this very ambiguous situation.

00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:22.415
So I just go down the path
and he's like, Where is the path?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.667
I like how

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:27.670
it transitions from super, super, super

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.798
loud and hectic to silent.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.801
The house is very discreet.

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:35.887
It's kind of hidden in the bushes,

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:39.015
very mysterious.

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:41.351
And this is actually a real building,
by the way.

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:42.811
It's a hotel in Norway.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:46.648
Yeah, the entire movie is shot inside
this hotel.

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:50.026
The house itself
almost becomes a character.

00:01:50.068 --> 00:01:53.780
His first encounter with
the CEO is actually his house.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.282
The house says a lot about the person.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:02:01.121
Actually, this cladding, if I'm not
mistaken, is a pine treated pine cladding.

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:04.499
Peter Zumthor
actually uses this in his home and office.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:10.088
The color of the cladding,
I think, adds to the dark tone.

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:13.424
You know, it's not the light wood color.

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:17.428
And you have this incredibly
heavy metal door,

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:21.349
the door is the connection
between the outside and inside.

00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:25.228
And I think the weight of the door
has a really big impact

00:02:25.228 --> 00:02:28.064
on how you perceive the space
when you go inside.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:31.901
And so it's already
creating this sense of mystery.

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:36.531
This detail is really nice here,
where the glass meets the stone,

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:40.785
the original architect
of the real building said they really,

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:43.788
really try
to focus on minimal intervention

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:47.333
to the site, and that's their approach
to sustainability.

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:51.087
This detail really speaks
to that philosophy.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:54.799
They're saying, no, we're not going
to touch the existing site.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:59.012
We're going to transform our building,
cut the glass

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:02.432
to meet all the jagged edges
of the existing stone.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.766
And you can see this detail

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:07.520
in Fallingwater by Frank Lloyd
Wright as well, which is probably

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.192
one of the most iconic integration
of architecture into nature.

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.861
Floor to ceiling, glass everywhere.

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:19.574
I think there's a really clever
use of glass in this entire movie.

00:03:19.616 --> 00:03:23.786
You've never been
outside this building.

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:28.791
you've never walked outside?

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:31.628
I've never been outside
 the room I am in now.

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:34.005
It really confuses you.

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:38.176
Sometimes it's almost impossible
to tell who's

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.888
the subject of the experiment
and who is the experimenter.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.601
And it also talks about a contrast
between confinement and freedom.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:50.146
The scene is interesting, too,
because I think this tree

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.982
has something very symbolic to this movie.

00:03:52.982 --> 00:03:58.029
When you're downstairs in the basement,
you have this very manicured

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:01.824
landscape, this little pot
with these two little trees.

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:06.120
And upstairs you have this expansive
nature all around you.

00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:09.791
Like if you compare
the use of glass upstairs to downstairs

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:15.129
upstairs,
the glass is used to enclose the space,

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:21.427
but at the same time provide views
to this expansive forest and to this lake.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:23.888
And you can look out into the mountains.

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:27.183
And then downstairs,
the glass is used to confine you.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:32.355
Actually, the original building
has frosted glass throughout

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:36.150
and I think they use CGI
to make it transparent.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.154
Or maybe they created a new set
for those in the real building.

00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:44.617
They're using the frosted glass
to create a create privacy

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:47.745
and create a sense of relaxation
and calmness.

00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:52.959
By turning all of the glass transparent,
you're able to see everything.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.086
So it's creating an
environment of surveillance.

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:03.428
five years ago, building on the research
of our predecessors at the Institute,

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:08.141
my team and I discovered a process
by which all organic material

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:11.561
can be reduced at the cellular level
by a ratio

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:16.190
of approximately 2744 to 1.

00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:19.986
Downsizing is a sci fi comedy drama,

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:24.824
and it's a movie that I like very much,
although it got pretty bad

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.910
reviews on Rotten Tomatoes,
I think it's really funny and smart.

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:32.540
So a group of scientists
invent this method

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:37.420
where you shrink the human body
into something about five inches.

00:05:37.962 --> 00:05:42.842
And this was their solution
to climate change and overpopulation.

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:46.679
By downsizing, you consume less material

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.849
and you in turn help the earth.

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:54.896
I hold here in my hand
all of the compostable waste

00:05:55.021 --> 00:06:00.485
produced by 36 people over four years.

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:03.946
The story is about Paul

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:07.950
Matt Damon, who,
you know, in his dire hours

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:12.163
kind of going through a midlife crisis,
decides to go through this procedure

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.873
and it's a non reversible.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:20.171
One of the benefits of downsizing, of
course, is that you can take your savings

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:26.260
and when you apply that to your downsized
life, your money,

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:30.473
because everything is so small, becomes
like millions and millions of dollars.

00:06:30.473 --> 00:06:34.811
And so you can live this very,
very lavish life in this tiny community.

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:36.604
And so, Paul,

00:06:36.604 --> 00:06:41.776
he decides to go through this procedure
with his wife, but when he actually

00:06:41.776 --> 00:06:46.239
shrinks himself, he ends up alone
because his wife ends up ditching him.

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.031
Last minute,

00:06:47.031 --> 00:06:48.658
I thought this movie
was really interesting

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:51.536
because it's focused inwards,
not outwards.

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:55.998
Like whenever we talk about
the climate crisis or of overpopulation,

00:06:56.332 --> 00:07:00.086
we're trying to change a bunch of things
in our environments.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:04.215
We're trying to build denser, we're trying
to build faster to house more people.

00:07:04.549 --> 00:07:10.096
But I really wonder what that process
was like for them to start researching.

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.098
Like, none of these solutions are working.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:15.101
We have to like drastically change
something about ourselves.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:19.272
This really reminded me of this book
that I read called Learning to Die

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:20.398
in the Anthropocene.

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:23.484
In this book,
the author, who's a war veteran,

00:07:23.484 --> 00:07:28.573
he lists out in detail
all the problems that we have.

00:07:28.573 --> 00:07:33.244
And he lists so in detail
all the solutions that we've created,

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:36.372
so renewable energy, solar, geothermal.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:41.586
And he outlines why none of these
solutions are ever going to work.

00:07:41.586 --> 00:07:47.800
And we are basically screwed
unless we drastically change

00:07:47.925 --> 00:07:51.846
the way we live
and the way we think and philosophically,

00:07:51.846 --> 00:07:56.392
like die as civilizations
and as individuals.

00:07:56.392 --> 00:07:59.395
And in this case, that drastic change

00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:03.274
was their physical change,
the shrunk their size.

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:08.321
What's more interesting and more ironic
is they've basically combined

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:12.742
the most sustainable solution
with the most unsustainable

00:08:12.742 --> 00:08:15.161
form of urban development, urban sprawl.

00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:20.458
All of these houses are,
you know, the American Dream mansions,

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:24.629
and they're these massive
gated communities, and they basically

00:08:24.629 --> 00:08:28.508
shrunk themselves so that they can consume
as little as possible,

00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:33.429
so that they can live the lavish lifestyle
that they've always wanted to live.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:37.600
In the end, they're not really changing
anything about themselves.

00:08:37.642 --> 00:08:39.727
They're just changing their size.

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:42.146
Yeah, it's
kind of ironic and kind of messed up

00:08:42.146 --> 00:08:45.233
because the first part of the movie
is them like

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:48.402
contemplating whether they should go
through the procedure or not.

00:08:48.402 --> 00:08:54.075
And do you understand that there exists
an approximately one in 225,000 chance

00:08:54.325 --> 00:08:57.161
that the procedure could result in injury,

00:08:57.161 --> 00:09:00.706
permanent disability or death?

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:03.709
When you look at

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:09.090
modern developments and ideas of healthier
living and healthier cities,

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:13.094
it's denser, like denser communities
with where everything is walkable

00:09:13.094 --> 00:09:14.804
and everything is bikeable.

00:09:14.804 --> 00:09:19.308
But it really makes you think
if you had all the money in the world

00:09:19.308 --> 00:09:22.395
and if you had millions
and millions of dollars to spend, would

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:26.524
we still be trying
to build these dense societies,

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:29.735
or will we just be sprawling out
everywhere?

