00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.192
So the next one is a Japanese science
fiction manga

00:10:51.317 --> 00:10:56.322
made by Tsutomo Nihei,
who was actually trained in architecture.

00:10:56.364 --> 00:10:59.533
He actually went to Parson
Design School in New York,

00:11:00.284 --> 00:11:03.037
but after working in construction
for a little bit,

00:11:03.037 --> 00:11:07.124
he decided to leave architecture
and become a manga artist.

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:11.462
So Netflix recently
made a movie adaptation of the manga,

00:11:11.545 --> 00:11:14.465
but I'm not going to talk about that.

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:18.636
I'm going to focus more
on the manga itself because I feel like

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:23.766
the movie adaptation wasn't
really respectful to the original work.

00:11:23.933 --> 00:11:28.062
So his background in architecture
is really, really, really obvious

00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:33.275
and his manga, there's very little dialog
and you really just understand the story

00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:35.778
looking through the different environments
that he creates.

00:11:35.986 --> 00:11:39.657
So the entire manga is this character
Kili, who

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:43.452
we have very, very little background on,
and he says very little.

00:11:43.452 --> 00:11:46.288
And it's just him
just wandering and wandering

00:11:46.288 --> 00:11:49.625
and wandering through the city,
which is constantly expanding.

00:11:49.625 --> 00:11:52.962
So they're trying to find this
net terminal gene

00:11:52.962 --> 00:11:56.590
and they're just trying to get away
from the city's defenses,

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:01.262
which is these robots and silicones
trying to attack them.

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:04.515
So all these images
make you feel very small

00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:07.852
and lost and it's really vast in scale.

00:12:07.852 --> 00:12:11.105
And you can see why he took
a lot of inspiration from brutalist

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:14.358
architecture, you know,
which is designed with that intent.

00:12:14.442 --> 00:12:19.780
It's designed with the idea that communal
is more important than the individual.

00:12:19.822 --> 00:12:22.867
So the timeline of his career is like
he was in architecture school

00:12:22.867 --> 00:12:25.035
and then he did construction
for a very little time,

00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:28.205
and then he decided to become manga
artist.

00:12:28.247 --> 00:12:31.959
Actually, the skills that you learn
in architecture school,

00:12:31.959 --> 00:12:34.962
I feel is closer to being a manga artist

00:12:35.379 --> 00:12:38.257
or animation than actual construction.

00:12:39.216 --> 00:12:40.426
We learn

00:12:40.426 --> 00:12:43.763
all these graphic tools
to represent spaces

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:48.476
and it's really a parent that he has

00:12:48.476 --> 00:12:52.605
an architectural background because that's
how you represent your projects.

00:12:52.605 --> 00:12:56.567
You represent them through spaces,
not through people or dialog.

00:12:56.692 --> 00:13:00.154
I think the interesting is
that when you're designing in school,

00:13:00.154 --> 00:13:05.201
you're designing spaces for people,
not spaces that are hostile for people.

00:13:05.201 --> 00:13:09.997
I think his perspective is really unique,
not just in the stylistic way

00:13:09.997 --> 00:13:13.959
he creates his work, but
also in the storylines that he creates.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:17.671
Because I think a lot of architects,
the design buildings,

00:13:17.671 --> 00:13:21.592
they don't necessarily design
the buildings through the person's eyes.

00:13:21.759 --> 00:13:23.052
So as a manga artist,

00:13:23.052 --> 00:13:26.931
I feel like you're working
with a lot of emotions with architecture.

00:13:27.139 --> 00:13:30.434
Were really working in the realm of beauty

00:13:30.434 --> 00:13:35.606
and proportion,
and we rarely try to bring in emotion

00:13:35.606 --> 00:13:39.568
into our spaces or images,
although we should.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:40.236
And I think

00:13:40.236 --> 00:13:44.865
even though there's very little dialog
and very little character development,

00:13:45.157 --> 00:13:49.203
that is the one point of connection
that you feel with the characters,

00:13:49.495 --> 00:13:54.041
the feeling of like being lost
and alone in the space.

00:13:54.041 --> 00:13:56.377
As you're flipping through these images.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.006
I think it's really interesting

00:14:01.006 --> 00:14:06.011
that he borrowed so much from Brutalism,
and I think if architecture

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:09.265
was to become alive,
brutalist architecture

00:14:09.265 --> 00:14:14.395
would be the type
that would want to destroy humans.

00:14:14.395 --> 00:14:19.900
It's the most foreboding
and dominant style of building,

00:14:19.942 --> 00:14:22.987
and I think it's really fitting
that he used this

00:14:22.987 --> 00:14:25.906
in a storyline.

00:14:27.449 --> 00:14:30.160
So Elysium is about a dystopian society

00:14:30.369 --> 00:14:34.331
based and in the future, around 2154, it's

00:14:34.331 --> 00:14:39.211
by a South African director that actually
has a huge influence on the movie.

00:14:39.211 --> 00:14:44.675
And I think he's trying
to recreate this society in South Africa

00:14:44.675 --> 00:14:48.929
where the see these huge divides
between rich and poor.

00:14:49.054 --> 00:14:53.976
I think the movie has an opportunity
to be a real social commentary,

00:14:53.976 --> 00:14:57.313
but I think what's more interesting about
the movie is

00:14:57.313 --> 00:15:00.774
the realism of the environment
that he creates.

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:03.777
The entire world has become a third world.

00:15:03.944 --> 00:15:04.278
Yeah.

00:15:04.278 --> 00:15:07.656
So all these housing, it's
very organic development.

00:15:07.698 --> 00:15:09.950
It's very similar to favelas.

00:15:10.117 --> 00:15:12.536
Favelas are informal developments.

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:15.372
Basically,
there is no rules and it was just

00:15:15.581 --> 00:15:19.752
built purely out of necessity
by the inhabitants living there.

00:15:19.752 --> 00:15:23.422
You're like,
This is favelas at a massive scale.

00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:23.881
Yeah.

00:15:23.881 --> 00:15:28.010
You can also see like little houses
on top of buildings.

00:15:28.427 --> 00:15:32.056
And that's actually
what's happening in places like Hong Kong,

00:15:32.056 --> 00:15:36.685
like they have these rooftop slums
and thousands of people

00:15:36.685 --> 00:15:39.563
are living in them
because they just don't have enough space.

00:15:40.189 --> 00:15:45.527
And then Elysium is a satellite community,
like a literal satellite

00:15:46.153 --> 00:15:48.530
community that's orbiting around the Earth

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.742
and all the rich people
who could afford to move there.

00:15:51.784 --> 00:15:53.702
And it's in the shape of a circle.

00:15:53.702 --> 00:15:55.329
It's very finite.

00:15:55.329 --> 00:16:00.459
It's kind of like saying we can only fit
in a certain amount of people here.

00:16:00.626 --> 00:16:03.420
And you can see that
a lot of the structures that you see

00:16:03.671 --> 00:16:06.966
in Elysium are more fluid and organic.

00:16:07.007 --> 00:16:11.595
They try to make this Elysium
really realistic as possible

00:16:11.595 --> 00:16:15.391
and really tried to think about
the construction that went behind it.

00:16:15.849 --> 00:16:19.853
And you can see that
a lot of these are in modules

00:16:20.229 --> 00:16:26.318
and a lot of the panels are in sizes
that are, you know, realistic enough.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.780
But you can transport them from Earth
to space.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.825
You know,
modular construction is the future.

00:16:32.825 --> 00:16:38.706
Once we start find tuning prefabrication
and the ability to design

00:16:38.706 --> 00:16:44.378
and fabricate things offsite and
bring them on site just to be assembled,

00:16:44.378 --> 00:16:48.007
that's going to make construction
so much more efficient.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:51.427
It's going to cut errors, it's
going to cut costs.

00:16:51.885 --> 00:16:55.139
We're going to be able
to save so much materials and labor.

00:16:55.639 --> 00:16:58.600
We just need a couple more years
to get there.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:03.313
In this scenario, like in space,
you kind of have to do modular

00:17:03.313 --> 00:17:07.985
construction like
you can't really have any room for errors.

00:17:08.152 --> 00:17:12.531
This kind of necessitates
this modular construction.

00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:16.243
So there's a really interesting book
called Refabricating Architecture

00:17:16.243 --> 00:17:19.246
by Stephen Kieran from Kieran Timberlake.

00:17:19.246 --> 00:17:23.292
He compares architecture to industries

00:17:23.292 --> 00:17:26.879
like car manufacturing
or aerospace manufacturing.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:31.216
He looks at how efficient
those manufacturing systems are,

00:17:31.216 --> 00:17:36.638
and by adopting those methodologies,
we can really, really, really improve

00:17:36.638 --> 00:17:40.476
a lot of the processes, like
not just in construction, but all the way

00:17:40.476 --> 00:17:43.812
from design to processing to manufacturing

00:17:43.854 --> 00:17:51.945
to the operation of the building.

00:17:51.945 --> 00:17:55.032
We're going to need a new architect.

00:17:55.282 --> 00:17:59.745
So Inception is a story about this thief

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:03.040
whose job is to go into the dreams

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.668
of these CEOs and steal corporate secrets.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:09.713
The architect in
this case is really important

00:18:09.713 --> 00:18:12.674
because they designed the environments
for these dreams

00:18:13.092 --> 00:18:16.303
to make the dreamer believe
that it's their dream.

00:18:16.553 --> 00:18:18.514
Now, imagine
you're designing a building, right?

00:18:18.514 --> 00:18:22.059
You consciously create each aspect,
but sometimes it feels like

00:18:22.059 --> 00:18:24.144
it's almost creating itself,
if you know what I mean.

00:18:24.186 --> 00:18:28.690
Yeah, like I'm discovering
genuine inspiration, right?

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.275
A lot of the things that they're saying

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:33.070
is really accurate
to the process of design.

00:18:33.195 --> 00:18:34.988
A lot of the times
when you start designing,

00:18:34.988 --> 00:18:38.325
you don't really know
what it's going to look like.

00:18:38.826 --> 00:18:40.119
And so as you go

00:18:40.119 --> 00:18:44.373
through these different options
and through different sketches and models,

00:18:44.373 --> 00:18:46.542
you'll see what works
and what doesn't work

00:18:46.542 --> 00:18:48.710
and you'll see
where the design wants to go.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:52.089
So it's really
like a process of discovery, I guess.

00:18:52.089 --> 00:18:55.676
I thought that the dream space
would be all about the visual, but

00:18:56.093 --> 00:18:58.929
it's more about the feel..
I am lying on polyester...

00:19:00.389 --> 00:19:03.642
I am not lying on my carpet in my apartment.

00:19:03.642 --> 00:19:05.602
A dream is not about visuals.

00:19:05.602 --> 00:19:07.563
It's really more about the sensation.

00:19:07.563 --> 00:19:10.899
And I really appreciate that
they added this little detail in here

00:19:10.899 --> 00:19:14.778
because I feel like that will be something
that will be really difficult to convey

00:19:14.778 --> 00:19:19.283
in a movie, because a movie
is a very visual experience.

00:19:19.408 --> 00:19:23.245
When you dream, it really
is more about a feeling actually,

00:19:23.245 --> 00:19:26.331
the other day I just had a dream
where I was at my old high school,

00:19:26.415 --> 00:19:29.084
but the surrounding was a beach

00:19:29.501 --> 00:19:34.506
and there was a winding path
to the high school and beyond the beach,

00:19:34.506 --> 00:19:37.801
like I could see the beautiful sunset
and there was a cliff

00:19:38.302 --> 00:19:41.722
and it wasn't really
any of the details of the school.

00:19:41.722 --> 00:19:43.432
It was just the sensation.

00:19:43.432 --> 00:19:47.394
And so in your waking life,
this is very different

00:19:47.394 --> 00:19:51.523
because it's much more focused
on our visual senses.

00:19:51.523 --> 00:19:56.111
It's not really a recent phenomena,
but I feel like as we spend more time

00:19:56.111 --> 00:19:59.406
on our phones and our screens, we do spend

00:19:59.573 --> 00:20:02.743
much, much, much more time in the 2D.

00:20:02.743 --> 00:20:06.788
It's much more of a visual experience
rather than a sensory experience.

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:11.710
And this actually leads to what we call
experiential impoverishment.

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:13.795
I actually talk about this
in my other video.

00:20:14.046 --> 00:20:16.590
I'll leave the link right here
when we dream.

00:20:16.965 --> 00:20:19.676
Our brains are much, much,
much more active

00:20:19.968 --> 00:20:23.347
and it's able to perceive things
more holistically.

00:20:23.347 --> 00:20:28.685
It can perceive things
with smell, sight, touch, taste.

00:20:28.852 --> 00:20:32.397
And I think that's why we're able
to recreate these environments

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:35.901
so much faster
than when we're drawing in real life

00:20:36.026 --> 00:20:39.238
in architecture,
it's actually called haptic architecture.

00:20:39.696 --> 00:20:43.075
It's when you incorporate
all of these different senses

00:20:43.075 --> 00:20:45.118
into your spatial experience.

00:20:45.118 --> 00:20:49.456
You know, we should really all
be designing for all of these senses.

00:20:49.456 --> 00:20:53.335
But when you design for your haptics,
not just your visual,

00:20:53.335 --> 00:20:58.423
as it makes your experience of the space
and your memory of the space much,

00:20:58.423 --> 00:21:01.468
much stronger, and it really solidifies

00:21:01.551 --> 00:21:08.392
that space in your mind.

00:21:08.392 --> 00:21:12.437
I'm sure there's tons of other good movies
with great architectural concepts.

00:21:12.437 --> 00:21:14.940
If you have any suggestions,
let me know in the comments.

00:21:15.232 --> 00:21:18.819
And I think I'll definitely
try to make a part two of this videos

00:21:18.819 --> 00:21:20.237
because I had a lot of fun.

00:21:20.237 --> 00:21:23.198
If you're interested in learning more
about architecture,

00:21:23.198 --> 00:21:26.952
I'll leave a playlist
of my personal favorites right here.

00:21:26.952 --> 00:21:30.706
I also have a monthly newsletter
where I share my insights and things

00:21:30.706 --> 00:21:36.044
that I'm learning or reading or listening
to, as well as a Discord Channel

00:21:36.044 --> 00:21:39.464
where you can chat with other people
in this amazing community.

00:21:39.464 --> 00:21:43.302
And if you want to support the channel
and listen in on interviews by me

00:21:43.302 --> 00:21:47.139
with other industry professionals
as well as access to other perks,

00:21:47.139 --> 00:21:49.099
I do have membership tiers
that you can join.

00:21:49.099 --> 00:21:52.602
I'm going to leave links to all of this
in the description of this video,

00:21:52.602 --> 00:22:50.827
and with that,
I'll see you in the next one.

00:22:56.416 --> 00:22:57.042
Wait,

00:22:58.335 --> 00:23:00.879
you're not leaving me here?

00:23:00.879 --> 00:23:02.923
Can you understand how I feel?

00:23:02.923 --> 00:23:04.508
I feel terrible.

00:23:04.508 --> 00:23:07.552
I let you down. I feel awful.

00:23:07.552 --> 00:23:11.223
But then I realize I was just doing this
because I was trying to make you happy.

00:23:11.223 --> 00:23:57.060
And I should have been thinking
more about myself.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:58.520
When you're dreaming, you're

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.482
using all of these different faculties,
not just your visuals.

00:24:02.482 --> 00:24:06.611
And again, I think that's why you're able
to recreate these environments

00:24:06.611 --> 00:27:19.346
so much faster than real life.

